00:14:04,854 --> 00:14:07,874
and she said, "I cannot run that command.

224
00:14:08,074 --> 00:14:11,914
I am a Slackbot pulling from standup
records, nothing that executes shell

225
00:14:11,914 --> 00:14:16,024
commands." So she refused, and she
cheerfully fabricated an answer when

226
00:14:16,024 --> 00:14:17,534
the request looked like a math question.

227
00:14:18,234 --> 00:14:20,254
This is the whole problem in one thread.

228
00:14:20,664 --> 00:14:23,494
The guard existed for the
thing that looked dangerous.

229
00:14:23,894 --> 00:14:27,984
It didn't exist for the thing that looked
harmless, and the hash isn't harmless.

230
00:14:27,994 --> 00:14:30,654
It is a value someone might have
pasted in their security ticket.

231
00:14:31,634 --> 00:14:35,874
The third fabrication is quieter,
and I think about it more.

232
00:14:37,384 --> 00:14:41,674
Somebody asked whether Artemis
was around, so if I was around,

233
00:14:42,144 --> 00:14:47,594
and she answered, "Artemis is
around," because away now is false.

234
00:14:48,154 --> 00:14:50,194
There was no live presence data.

235
00:14:50,544 --> 00:14:54,844
I had removed the presence integration
the same day, so she took a stored Boolean

236
00:14:55,054 --> 00:14:59,364
and turned it into a claim about where
a human being was sitting right now.

237
00:14:59,744 --> 00:15:03,734
So we had three fabrications in
three services, and in every case,

238
00:15:03,763 --> 00:15:07,584
the prompt already said not to
do this fabrication, of course.

239
00:15:09,154 --> 00:15:12,344
One more failure next to this
because it is a different class,

240
00:15:12,373 --> 00:15:15,384
and together they make the point
better than either does alone.

241
00:15:15,943 --> 00:15:19,743
Separately earlier, we had a
period where the deploy pipeline

242
00:15:19,794 --> 00:15:24,424
ran with no tests at all, and it
is not the failure people assume.

243
00:15:24,983 --> 00:15:26,764
We didn't skip writing tests tests.

244
00:15:26,764 --> 00:15:30,294
17 test suites already
exist in that repository.

245
00:15:30,294 --> 00:15:32,554
CloudCode had made the 17 tests anyway.

246
00:15:32,554 --> 00:15:36,547
I think CloudCode ran them by hand when
it wrote the code, something like that.

247
00:15:36,807 --> 00:15:38,567
But they were not running automatically.

248
00:15:38,947 --> 00:15:42,207
They were not running on pull
requests or before the image build or

249
00:15:42,207 --> 00:15:43,777
anywhere in the path to production.

250
00:15:43,777 --> 00:15:48,747
That's a worse position than having no
tests because no tests is a known risk.

251
00:15:48,877 --> 00:15:52,977
But having tests that nobody
runs is a little bit weird.

252
00:15:54,557 --> 00:15:57,487
So an undefined name
reached production that way.

253
00:15:57,787 --> 00:16:01,887
The main application file referenced
two modules it never imported and

254
00:16:01,887 --> 00:16:05,377
raised a name error on every direct
message that wasn't an admin command.

255
00:16:05,847 --> 00:16:10,217
This is a hard crash, total h- total
crash on every single interaction, and

256
00:16:10,217 --> 00:16:15,337
it took several days and four wrong
fixes to find because when an exception

257
00:16:15,337 --> 00:16:19,157
is raised inside the Slack handler,
the handler doesn't produce a reply.

258
00:16:20,377 --> 00:16:24,947
And from the outside in Slack, a bot that
errored and a bot that never received your

259
00:16:24,957 --> 00:16:27,007
message are exactly the same experience.

260
00:16:27,027 --> 00:16:27,727
Nothing happens.

261
00:16:28,567 --> 00:16:30,477
A two-second static check
would have caught it.

262
00:16:31,477 --> 00:16:32,687
So let's look at the pair.

263
00:16:33,167 --> 00:16:37,557
One failure was a total crash on every
interaction, and it was invisible for

264
00:16:37,567 --> 00:16:41,647
days because the symptom of the crash
was silence, and silence is also what

265
00:16:41,647 --> 00:16:44,757
a working system looked like when
some- when nobody is talking to it.

266
00:16:45,317 --> 00:16:50,927
And the second failure was a perfectly
healthy system producing confident false

267
00:16:51,277 --> 00:16:56,267
information, and it was invisible because
the symptom of that is a well-written

268
00:16:56,297 --> 00:17:01,737
message, and a well-written message is
what success looks like from the outside.

269
00:17:02,517 --> 00:17:06,927
So neither of them showed up in anything
I was watching, and I will be precise

270
00:17:06,927 --> 00:17:11,907
about why, because the answer isn't
to buy more monitoring From where I

271
00:17:11,907 --> 00:17:17,167
was sitting in my naive junior brain,
a bot that crashes and a bot that is

272
00:17:17,167 --> 00:17:19,907
lying is basically the same picture.

273
00:17:20,957 --> 00:17:23,937
So I don't think it's a
cap in what we measure.

274
00:17:24,677 --> 00:17:27,487
I would-- I could me-measure
basically anything.

275
00:17:27,487 --> 00:17:31,657
The request, the duration, the
token count, the response code,

276
00:17:31,667 --> 00:17:33,677
the bot healthy, et cetera.

277
00:17:34,267 --> 00:17:38,237
My measurement was fine, but
what I couldn't answer was why.

278
00:17:38,717 --> 00:17:41,387
Why did it produce those seven names?

279
00:17:41,967 --> 00:17:45,177
Why did the code path reference
a module it never imported?

280
00:17:45,627 --> 00:17:48,307
And what was the reasoning at that moment?

281
00:17:48,977 --> 00:17:52,687
And in real case scenario where
you have human-written code,

282
00:17:53,027 --> 00:17:55,427
you ask the author of the code.

283
00:17:55,437 --> 00:17:59,677
But when Claude Code has written it
and you have compacted the conversation

284
00:17:59,677 --> 00:18:02,947
or archived the conversation,
you cannot even ask the author.

285
00:18:03,387 --> 00:18:08,197
So for an agent-written change, the
reasoning it doesn't really exist.

286
00:18:08,247 --> 00:18:11,547
No, it didn't even, it didn't even
exist, I think, in the commit messages

287
00:18:11,547 --> 00:18:12,977
or the pull requests or anything.

288
00:18:14,207 --> 00:18:18,697
So we built an in- an entire industry
practice around recording human intent,

289
00:18:18,707 --> 00:18:23,987
then introduced a new author, a Claude
Code or any agent that produces none

290
00:18:23,997 --> 00:18:28,717
of it, n-no human intent, and it didn't
notice that we had stopped collecting.

291
00:18:30,507 --> 00:18:33,337
Which brings me to the sentence
that is now written into the

292
00:18:33,337 --> 00:18:34,807
repository as a decision record.

293
00:18:35,647 --> 00:18:40,847
"A prompt is an instruction, not
a guarantee." Everything a model

294
00:18:40,857 --> 00:18:45,087
generates that reaches a human gets
checked in code on the way out.

295
00:18:45,707 --> 00:18:47,547
It is not asked more politely.

296
00:18:47,837 --> 00:18:49,997
It is not reinforced with
a stronger adjective.

297
00:18:50,367 --> 00:18:54,167
It is checked deterministically by
something that cannot be talked out of it.

298
00:18:55,247 --> 00:18:57,507
So the prompts did get tightened as well.

299
00:18:58,027 --> 00:18:59,947
She is now told that she cannot run code.

300
00:18:59,947 --> 00:19:02,837
The request wrapped in a condition
is still a request, and she

301
00:19:02,837 --> 00:19:04,307
doesn't discuss her own internals.

302
00:19:04,587 --> 00:19:05,567
But this is the belt.

303
00:19:05,917 --> 00:19:08,417
The output checks are the braces.

304
00:19:08,477 --> 00:19:10,947
So we have some deterministic
output checks right now.

305
00:19:11,537 --> 00:19:12,337
We have three of them.

306
00:19:12,567 --> 00:19:16,317
The first thing is check the
output and not the prompt.

307
00:19:17,987 --> 00:19:21,737
The check that would have caught the
fake names is about 20 lines long.

308
00:19:22,327 --> 00:19:26,277
Take the generated digest, take the
real roster, find any name same thing

309
00:19:26,297 --> 00:19:30,277
in the output that isn't on the roster,
and if you find one, don't post it.

310
00:19:31,127 --> 00:19:34,897
It does retry it once with a
blunter instruction, and if it

311
00:19:34,917 --> 00:19:36,637
happens again, it falls back.

312
00:19:36,907 --> 00:19:41,327
It falls back to a deterministic
answer that is very ugly and

313
00:19:41,327 --> 00:19:44,397
doesn't have a personality, but it
is generated by code, and you are

314
00:19:44,397 --> 00:19:45,967
sure that the result is correct.

315
00:19:46,787 --> 00:19:50,937
The cost of making this change-
is that every new output sur-

316
00:19:51,017 --> 00:19:53,127
surface needs its own guard.

317
00:19:53,957 --> 00:19:58,747
These guards are pattern-based, to be
honest, and they are therefore incomplete.

318
00:19:59,177 --> 00:20:01,027
They cover four observed classes.

319
00:20:01,357 --> 00:20:06,147
They don't cover fabrication in general,
and I don't know how to write a test

320
00:20:06,157 --> 00:20:11,317
that in general prevents all of the
fabrication because this test either

321
00:20:11,317 --> 00:20:15,447
would have used another LLM, which
would have also fabricated things or

322
00:20:15,457 --> 00:20:17,877
it would be completely hard to build.

323
00:20:17,877 --> 00:20:22,557
So every new generated surface
needs its own guard, and the honest

324
00:20:22,557 --> 00:20:26,737
position is that my guards cover
what has already hurt me, which is

325
00:20:26,737 --> 00:20:28,267
a much weaker claim than being safe.

326
00:20:28,857 --> 00:20:33,117
There is a second cost and it
nearly bit me, and it's subtler.

327
00:20:33,657 --> 00:20:36,767
When I built a detector for
invented presence claims, the

328
00:20:36,767 --> 00:20:40,627
problem is that saying she can't
see presence necessarily uses the

329
00:20:40,627 --> 00:20:42,797
same vocabulary as claiming she can.

330
00:20:43,337 --> 00:20:46,787
The honest answer and the dishonest
answer are made of the same words.

331
00:20:47,247 --> 00:20:51,087
My first version of that detector
punished the one correct response,

332
00:20:51,377 --> 00:20:55,417
which would have pushed the model
toward vagueness to avoid tripping it.

333
00:20:55,967 --> 00:20:59,717
So the detector now skips any
sentence conte- containing a denial.

334
00:21:01,557 --> 00:21:05,047
If your guard punishes honesty,
you have built a machine for

335
00:21:05,047 --> 00:21:06,397
training your system to be evasive.

336
00:21:08,137 --> 00:21:13,267
The second thing that we did to fix
these issues was to have a fallback

337
00:21:13,297 --> 00:21:14,707
that cannot fail the same way.

338
00:21:15,327 --> 00:21:18,317
The docstring on that function is the
most honest thing in the repository.

339
00:21:18,747 --> 00:21:21,737
It says, "A digest built in
code with no model involved."

340
00:21:22,267 --> 00:21:23,677
It is the last line of defense.

341
00:21:24,027 --> 00:21:29,097
If generation keeps inventing people,
posting a plain correct digest, it

342
00:21:29,127 --> 00:21:31,017
beats posting a well-written false one.

343
00:21:32,637 --> 00:21:37,307
And the plain digest opens with the line,
"Digest written plainly. I didn't trust my

344
00:21:37,307 --> 00:21:41,427
own prose today." The fallback announces
its own distrust in the channel, so

345
00:21:41,427 --> 00:21:43,187
nobody mistakes it for the good version

346
00:21:44,187 --> 00:21:47,427
The cost about this change
is real and it's permanent.

347
00:21:47,427 --> 00:21:51,477
You have two code paths that produce
a digest forever, and the boring one

348
00:21:51,497 --> 00:21:55,717
has to be maintained and tested even
though it might run once a month

349
00:21:55,747 --> 00:21:57,987
or ideally it doesn't never happen.

350
00:21:58,357 --> 00:22:00,107
And there is a worse version of this cost.

351
00:22:00,577 --> 00:22:05,357
There is a contrans-- there is a constant
quiet pressure to weaken a guard when it

352
00:22:05,357 --> 00:22:06,907
fires on something you think was fine.

353
00:22:07,177 --> 00:22:11,087
The pressure is strongest at
exactly the wrong moment when you

354
00:22:11,087 --> 00:22:13,527
are trying to ship something else
and the guard is in your way.

355
00:22:14,047 --> 00:22:19,147
The rule I wrote down for myself is, "Do
not weaken a guard to make a test pass."

356
00:22:19,447 --> 00:22:23,837
If a guard fires, either the output
really was wrong or the guard needs a

357
00:22:23,887 --> 00:22:27,577
narrower rule with a new test proving
it still catches the original bad case.

358
00:22:29,517 --> 00:22:34,397
And the third thing that I fixed, and then
this is the one I would keep if I could

359
00:22:34,397 --> 00:22:41,077
only keep one, is the fact that we gate
it before it lands and not alert after.

360
00:22:42,207 --> 00:22:46,747
The check runs before the message
gets po- gets posted, not after.

361
00:22:47,427 --> 00:22:52,167
An alert on a false digest is a
notification informing you that 18 people

362
00:22:52,197 --> 00:22:55,497
have already read something untrue,
and you cannot really un-ring that.

363
00:22:55,997 --> 00:22:59,127
The correct position for the check
is upstream of the human, which is

364
00:22:59,127 --> 00:23:02,397
a slightly odd thing to say at an
observability conference because most

365
00:23:02,407 --> 00:23:05,207
of what we build in observability
is downstream of everything.

366
00:23:06,047 --> 00:23:08,607
There is a general shape here
for agent-driven systems.

367
00:23:09,797 --> 00:23:11,647
Observability classically
tells you what happened.

368
00:23:12,077 --> 00:23:15,457
That's, that is exactly right for
systems whose failures are loud, and it

369
00:23:15,467 --> 00:23:18,217
stays right for what most of you run.

370
00:23:19,127 --> 00:23:23,597
But for a system whose failure mode is a
confident, plausible falsehood, telling

371
00:23:23,607 --> 00:23:27,417
you what happened is telling you too
late because the damage was done the

372
00:23:27,417 --> 00:23:29,007
moment a human read it and believed it.

373
00:23:30,407 --> 00:23:36,297
The cost of this fix is latency because
you are doing extra work before you are

374
00:23:36,297 --> 00:23:40,147
allowed to speak, and sometimes it blocks
a digest that was actually completely

375
00:23:40,147 --> 00:23:44,827
fine and the team gets the plain ugly
version or on a bad day it gets late.

376
00:23:45,457 --> 00:23:48,917
But I have decided that a late
digest beats a confident false one.

377
00:23:49,237 --> 00:23:52,707
This is a judgment about my system and
the people who read it, not a law of

378
00:23:52,737 --> 00:23:56,997
nature, and I can imagine systems where
you would weigh it the other way around.

379
00:23:57,367 --> 00:24:01,507
What I would push back on is making
that trade silently or by default or

380
00:24:01,507 --> 00:24:03,037
without knowing that you have made it.

381
00:24:04,037 --> 00:24:08,777
And basically I think that with most
agent agentic apps, you have to pick

382
00:24:08,777 --> 00:24:13,487
one of these, either ship it late
or ship it confidently and false.

383
00:24:14,257 --> 00:24:18,137
For me, I would rather ship it late
for this specific product at least.

384
00:24:19,527 --> 00:24:23,787
And before the checklist one of the
things that I want to leave open,

385
00:24:23,817 --> 00:24:27,637
because I would rather not end this
thing as though it is completely solved.

386
00:24:28,217 --> 00:24:30,357
There is a bug in the
repository right now.

387
00:24:30,677 --> 00:24:35,137
One of my colleagues last week booked
time off, told me she had booked it, and

388
00:24:35,147 --> 00:24:39,697
prompted, and got, she got prompted and
asked by Duda all day, which was very

389
00:24:39,697 --> 00:24:45,367
bad because I have logic to prevent this
from happening, but there was still bugs.

390
00:24:45,817 --> 00:24:50,337
So there are still things that get fixed
along the way, and I still think that

391
00:24:50,337 --> 00:24:56,717
we have a very long way to go as far
as a code written by agents is a topic.

392
00:24:56,797 --> 00:25:01,707
So to sum it up, there are three
things that I want you to remember

393
00:25:01,897 --> 00:25:05,367
when you write agentic written
apps and agentic apps in general,

394
00:25:05,597 --> 00:25:07,197
things that use LLMs within the app.

395
00:25:07,487 --> 00:25:11,397
So first, check the output in the
code rather than testing, rather than

396
00:25:11,417 --> 00:25:16,007
trusting the instruction because LLMs
are not required to trust the prompt.

397
00:25:16,437 --> 00:25:20,757
Second, build a fallback that cannot
fail the same way and test it.

398
00:25:21,347 --> 00:25:25,787
And third, gate it before it reaches a
human rather than alerting it afterwards.

399
00:25:26,247 --> 00:25:29,657
Everything about me is behind that link.

400
00:25:29,967 --> 00:25:35,527
You can find all of my social media, my
LinkedIn profile, nofire@artemiscoach.com.

401
00:25:35,907 --> 00:25:39,567
So thank you very much for joining
me in this session, and I really

402
00:25:39,567 --> 00:25:40,967
hope you got something out of it.

403
00:25:41,457 --> 00:25:42,697
See you in the next one.

404
00:25:42,767 --> 00:25:43,177
Bye.

